[MV2 MV-WEAK — the target: are the projections sigma-determined?]
  sigma is a FULL-VIEW abstraction.  MV1 shows p/r candidates read
  the full-view values, so those are sigma-determined outright.
  The whole question therefore collapses onto the IDLE weight,
  which reads has_p / has_r on the bare cone.
  full-view (has_p, has_r) -> set of cone-level values observed:
    (False, False) -> [(False, True), (True, False)]
    (False, True) -> [(False, True)]
    (True, False) -> [(True, False)]
  [PASS] MV2 THE 2-BIT PROXY IS NOT A FUNCTION — reported as the negative it is, with its MECHANISM.  The cone-level (has_p, has_r) an idle reads is NOT determined by the full-view (has_p, has_r): full-view (False, False) maps to BOTH (False, True) and (True, False).  **A SMALLER VIEW CAN YIELD MORE OPTIONS**, because prop_options_in_view excludes a base on which the actor already has a live proposal — so a view that MISSES that proposal INCLUDES the base.  Monotonicity fails, and any argument assuming a lagged view sees a subset of the options is wrong.  This does NOT refute MV-WEAK: sigma records far more than these two bits, and MV3 tests the real thing  (map = {(False, False): [(False, True), (True, False)], (False, True): [(False, True)], (True, False): [(True, False)]} — the (False, False) fibre is multi-valued, which is the finding)

